<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Palace State of Phaistos</h1><p class="st-meta">1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.) · capital Phaistos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Minoan palace centre of the Mesara plain in southern Crete, famous for its grand staircases and the enigmatic Phaistos Disc.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a> stands on a ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara</a>, the largest plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, which had been settled since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. Around 1950 BCE its first palace was built around a paved central court, and Phaistos became the leading power of southern Crete. After destructions in the 18th century BCE the palace was rebuilt on a grander scale.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a> controlled the agricultural wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara</a> and probably the nearby port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kommos">Kommos</a>, which traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Its archives of clay sealings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> tablets reveal a developed palace bureaucracy. Finds include fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamares-ware">Kamares ware</a> pottery and the stamped clay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc">Phaistos Disc</a>, whose script is still undeciphered. The nearby villa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a> was closely linked to it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The palace was destroyed c. 1450 BCE and its role passed largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a> and to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Knossos</a>, which came to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> under Mycenaean rulers. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a> later revived as a Greek city-state, described separately as Phaistos. The site was excavated by Italian archaeologists from 1900.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:20.90%;width:9.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1950 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century-bce">15th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phaistos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Minoan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Minoan (undeciphered)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phaistos, Minoan Phaistos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1700&amp;focus=palace-state-of-phaistos" aria-label="Palace State of Phaistos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5309 910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palace State of Phaistos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5629 1166L5637 1158"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5637 1158h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5629 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5629 1166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phaistos. <a href="/atlas?year=-1700&amp;focus=palace-state-of-phaistos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

